@noindent
@code{mcons} builds new list structure by creating new pairs, while
@code{set_car} and @code{set_cdr} modify existing pairs.  Indeed, we could
implement @code{mcons} in terms of the two mutators, together with a
procedure @code{get_new_pair}, which returns a new pair that is not part of
any existing list structure.  We obtain the new pair, set its @code{car} and
@code{cdr} fields to the designated objects, and return the new pair as the
result of @code{mcons}.@footnote{@code{get_new_pair} would be one of the
operations implemented as part of a Lisp's memory management, discussed in
@ref{5.3.1}; here OCaml's own allocator plays that role, and the record
literal below is the request for a fresh block.}

@example ocaml
let cons_from_mutators x y =
  let new_pair = { car = mnil; cdr = mnil } in
  set_car (Pair new_pair) x;
  set_cdr (Pair new_pair) y;
  Pair new_pair
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.12}Exercise 3.12:} The following procedure for
appending lists was introduced in @ref{2.2.1}:

@example ocaml
let rec append x y =
  match x with
  | Nil -> y
  | Pair _ -> mcons (car x) (append (cdr x) y)
  | _ -> invalid_arg "append: not a list"
@end example

@code{append} forms a new list by successively @code{mcons}ing the elements
of @code{x} onto @code{y}.  The procedure @code{append_bang} is similar to
@code{append}, but it is a mutator rather than a constructor.  It appends the
lists by splicing them together, modifying the final pair of @code{x} so that
its @code{cdr} is now @code{y}.  (It is an error to call @code{append_bang}
with an empty @code{x}.)

@example ocaml
let append_bang x y =
  match x with
  | Nil -> invalid_arg "append!: the first list is empty"
  | _ ->
    set_cdr (last_pair x) y;
    x
@end example

Here @code{last_pair} is a procedure that returns the last pair in its
argument:

@example ocaml
let rec last_pair x = if is_pair (cdr x) then last_pair (cdr x) else x
@end example

Consider the interaction

@example ocaml
let x = from_symbols [ "a"; "b" ]
let y = from_symbols [ "c"; "d" ]
let z = append x y

show z
(* "(a b c d)" *)

show (cdr x)
⟨@var{response}⟩

let w = append_bang x y

show w
(* "(a b c d)" *)

show (cdr x)
⟨@var{response}⟩
@end example

What are the missing @code{⟨}@var{response}@code{⟩}s?  Draw box-and-pointer diagrams to
explain your answer.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.13}Exercise 3.13:} Consider the following
@code{make_cycle} procedure, which uses the @code{last_pair} procedure defined
in @ref{Exercise 3.12}:

@example ocaml
let make_cycle x =
  set_cdr (last_pair x) x;
  x
@end example

Draw a box-and-pointer diagram that shows the structure @code{z} created by

@example ocaml
let z = make_cycle (from_symbols [ "a"; "b"; "c" ])
@end example

What happens if we try to compute @code{last_pair z}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{Exercise 3.13a is added by this edition and extends exercise 3.13; SICP
numbers stop at 3.13.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.13a}Exercise 3.13a:}
Exercise 3.13 asks what happens when @code{last_pair} meets a cycle: the walk
never reaches an empty @code{cdr}, so the call never returns.  The same fate
awaits @code{show} from @ref{3.3.1}, which also walks @code{cdr} chains to
build its printed string.  Write @code{show_cycle}, a variant of @code{show}
that never diverges: it remembers every pair it has visited, by physical
identity, and prints @code{#cycle} the moment it revisits one instead of
walking it again.  A structure that is merely large rather than cyclic should
still print normally, so give @code{show_cycle} a @code{fuel} budget that
raises rather than hangs once exhausted.  Check your printer against
@code{make_cycle (from_symbols [ "a"; "b"; "c" ])} and against a plain,
acyclic list.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.14}Exercise 3.14:} The following procedure is quite
useful, although obscure:

@example ocaml
let mystery x =
  let rec loop x y =
    if not (is_pair x)
    then y
    else (
      let temp = cdr x in
      set_cdr x y;
      loop temp x)
  in
  loop x mnil
@end example

@code{loop} uses the ``temporary'' variable @code{temp} to hold the old value
of the @code{cdr} of @code{x}, since the @code{set_cdr} on the next line
destroys the @code{cdr}.  Explain what @code{mystery} does in general.  Suppose
@code{v} is bound to @code{from_symbols [ "a"; "b"; "c"; "d" ]}. Draw the
box-and-pointer diagram that represents the list to which @code{v} is bound.
Suppose that we now evaluate @code{let w = mystery v}. Draw
box-and-pointer diagrams that show the structures @code{v} and @code{w} after
evaluating this expression.  What would @code{show} print for @code{v}
and @code{w}?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sharing and identity

We mentioned in @ref{3.1.3} the theoretical issues of ``sameness'' and
``change'' raised by the introduction of assignment.  These issues arise in
practice when individual pairs are @newterm{shared} among different data
objects.  For example, consider the structure formed by

@example ocaml
let x = from_symbols [ "a"; "b" ]
let z1 = mcons x x
@end example

@noindent
As shown in @ref{Figure 3.16}, @code{z1} is a pair whose @code{car} and
@code{cdr} both point to the same pair @code{x}.  This sharing of @code{x} by
the @code{car} and @code{cdr} of @code{z1} is a consequence of the
straightforward way in which @code{mcons} is implemented: it stores whatever
value @code{x} already designates in both fields, so referring to the one
bound name twice hands both fields the very same pointer.  In general, using
@code{mcons} to construct lists will result in an interlinked structure of
pairs in which many individual pairs are shared by many different structures.

