@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.16}Exercise 3.16:} Ben Bitdiddle decides to write a
procedure to count the number of pairs in any list structure.  ``It's easy,''
he reasons.  ``The number of pairs in any structure is the number in the
@code{car} plus the number in the @code{cdr} plus one more to count the current
pair.''  So Ben writes the following procedure:

@example ocaml
let rec count_pairs x =
  if is_pair x then count_pairs (car x) + count_pairs (cdr x) + 1 else 0
@end example

Show that this procedure is not correct.  In particular, draw box-and-pointer
diagrams representing list structures made up of exactly three pairs for which
Ben's procedure would return 3; return 4; return 7; never return at all.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.17}Exercise 3.17:} Devise a correct version of the
@code{count_pairs} procedure of @ref{Exercise 3.16} that returns the number of
distinct pairs in any structure.  (Hint: Traverse the structure, maintaining an
auxiliary data structure that is used to keep track of which pairs have already
been counted.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.18}Exercise 3.18:} Write a procedure that examines a
list and determines whether it contains a cycle, that is, whether a program
that tried to find the end of the list by taking successive @code{cdr}s would
go into an infinite loop.  @ref{Exercise 3.13} constructed such lists.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.19}Exercise 3.19:} Redo @ref{Exercise 3.18} using an
algorithm that takes only a constant amount of space.  (This requires a very
clever idea.)
@end quotation

@subsubheading Mutation is just assignment

When we introduced compound data, we observed in @ref{2.1.3} that pairs
can be represented purely in terms of procedures.  Read
@code{p.pcar} and @code{p.pcdr} as the @code{car} and @code{cdr} of the
procedural pair @code{p}: the pair is a record of closures over two cells.

@example ocaml
let proc_cons x y =
  let x = ref x in
  let y = ref y in
  { pcar = (fun () -> !x); pcdr = (fun () -> !y) }
@end example

@noindent
The same observation is true for mutable data.  We can implement mutable data
objects as procedures using assignment and local state.  For instance, we can
extend the above pair implementation to handle @code{set_car} and
@code{set_cdr} in a manner analogous to the way we implemented bank accounts
using @code{make_account} in @ref{3.1.1}:

@example ocaml
let proc_cons x y =
  let x = ref x in
  let y = ref y in
  { pcar = (fun () -> !x); pcdr = (fun () -> !y)
  ; set_pcar = (fun v -> x := v); set_pcdr = (fun v -> y := v) }
@end example

@noindent
Assignment is all that is needed, theoretically, to account for the behavior of
mutable data.  As soon as we admit assignment to our language, we raise all
the issues, not only of assignment, but of mutable data in general.@footnote{On
the other hand, from the viewpoint of implementation, assignment requires us to
modify the environment, which is itself a mutable data structure.  Thus,
assignment and mutation are equipotent: Each can be implemented in terms of the
other.}

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 3.20 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it;
the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.20}Exercise 3.20:}
Suppose we evaluate

@example ocaml
let x = proc_cons (Int 1) (Int 2)
let z = proc_cons x x

(z.pcdr ()).set_pcar (Int 17)
@end example

@noindent
Trace this sequence through the closures above.  In particular: what does
@code{x.pcar ()} return afterwards, and are @code{z.pcar ()} and
@code{z.pcdr ()} physically the same object as @code{x}?  (Compare
@ref{Exercise 3.11}.)
@end quotation

@node	3.3.2, 3.3.3, 3.3.1, 3.3
@subsection Representing Queues

The mutators @code{set_car} and @code{set_cdr} enable us to use pairs to
construct data structures that cannot be built with @code{mcons}, @code{car},
and @code{cdr} alone.  This section shows how to use pairs to represent a data
structure called a queue.  Section @ref{3.3.3} will show how to represent data
structures called tables.

A @newterm{queue} is a sequence in which items are inserted at one end (called
the @newterm{rear} of the queue) and deleted from the other end (the
@newterm{front}).  @ref{Figure 3.18} shows an initially empty queue in which
the items @code{a} and @code{b} are inserted.  Then @code{a} is removed,
@code{c} and @code{d} are inserted, and @code{b} is removed.  Because items are
always removed in the order in which they are inserted, a queue is sometimes
called a @newterm{FIFO} (first in, first out) buffer.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.18}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.

@example
Operation                    Resulting Queue
let q = make_queue ()
insert_queue q (Sym "a")     a
insert_queue q (Sym "b")     a b
delete_queue q               b
insert_queue q (Sym "c")     b c
insert_queue q (Sym "d")     b c d
delete_queue q               c d
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.18,,119mm,Figure 3.18: Queue operations.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.18.std,,119mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.}
@end float

@noindent
In terms of data abstraction, we can regard a queue as defined by the following
set of operations:

@itemize @bullet

@item
a constructor: @code{make_queue ()} returns an empty queue (a queue containing
no items).

@item
two selectors:

@example ocaml
empty_queue ⟨@var{queue}⟩
@end example

@noindent
tests if the queue is empty.

@example ocaml
front_queue ⟨@var{queue}⟩
@end example

@noindent
returns the object at the front of the queue, signaling an error if the queue
is empty; it does not modify the queue.

@item
two mutators:

@example ocaml
insert_queue ⟨@var{queue}⟩ ⟨@var{item}⟩
@end example

@noindent
inserts the item at the rear of the queue and returns the modified queue as its
value.

@example ocaml
delete_queue ⟨@var{queue}⟩
@end example

@noindent
removes the item at the front of the queue and returns the modified queue as
its value, signaling an error if the queue is empty before the deletion.

@end itemize

@noindent
Because a queue is a sequence of items, we could certainly represent it as an
ordinary list; the front of the queue would be the @code{car} of the list,
inserting an item in the queue would amount to appending a new element at the
end of the list, and deleting an item from the queue would just be taking the
rest of the list.  However, this representation is inefficient, because
in order to insert an item we must scan the list until we reach the end.  Since
the only method we have for scanning a list is by successive @code{cdr} field
reads, this scanning requires @math{{\Theta(n)}} steps for a list of @math{n}
items.  A simple modification to the list representation overcomes this
disadvantage by allowing the queue operations to be implemented so that they
require @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps; that is, so that the number of steps needed is
independent of the length of the queue.

The difficulty with the list representation arises from the need to scan to
find the end of the list.  The reason we need to scan is that, although the
standard way of representing a list as a chain of pairs readily provides us
with a pointer to the beginning of the list, it gives us no easily accessible
pointer to the end.  The modification that avoids the drawback is to represent
the queue as a list, together with an additional pointer that indicates the
final pair in the list.  That way, when we go to insert an item, we can consult
the rear pointer and so avoid scanning the list.

@noindent
A queue is represented, then, as a pair of pointers, @code{front_ptr} and
@code{rear_ptr}, which indicate, respectively, the first and last pairs in an
ordinary list.  Since we would like the queue to be an identifiable object, we
make the queue itself one mutable pair whose @code{car} is the front pointer
and whose @code{cdr} is the rear pointer.  @ref{Figure 3.19} illustrates this
representation.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.19}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+-------------------+
       +-|-+---+                   |
         |                         |
         | front-ptr               | rear-ptr
         V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |
     +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.19b,,118mm,Figure 3.19: Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.19b.std,,118mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.}
@end float

To define the queue operations we use the following procedures, which enable us
to select and to modify the front and rear pointers of a queue:

@example ocaml
let front_ptr (q : t) = q.Mpairs.car
let rear_ptr (q : t) = q.Mpairs.cdr

let set_front_ptr (q : t) item = q.Mpairs.car <- item
let set_rear_ptr (q : t) item = q.Mpairs.cdr <- item
@end example

@noindent
Now we can implement the actual queue operations.  We will consider a queue to
be empty if its front pointer is the empty list:

@example ocaml
let empty_queue (q : t) = Mpairs.Nil = front_ptr q
@end example

@noindent
The @code{make_queue} constructor returns, as an initially empty queue, a pair
whose @code{car} and @code{cdr} are both the empty list:

@example ocaml
let make_queue () : t = { Mpairs.car = Mpairs.Nil; cdr = Mpairs.Nil }
@end example

@noindent
To select the item at the front of the queue, we return the @code{car} of the
pair indicated by the front pointer:

@example ocaml
let front_queue q =
  if empty_queue q
  then invalid_arg "FRONT called with an empty queue"
  else Mpairs.car (front_ptr q)
@end example

@noindent
To insert an item in a queue, we follow the method whose result is indicated in
@ref{Figure 3.20}.  We first create a new pair whose @code{car} is the item to
be inserted and whose @code{cdr} is the empty list.  If the queue was initially
empty, we set the front and rear pointers of the queue to this new pair.
Otherwise, we modify the final pair in the queue to point to the new pair, and
also set the rear pointer to the new pair.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.20}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{insert_queue q (Sym "d")} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         |                                      |
         | front-ptr                            | rear-ptr
         V                                      V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.20c,,141mm,Figure 3.20: Result of using insert_queue q (Sym "d") on the queue of Figure 3.19.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.20c.std,,141mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{insert_queue q (Sym "d")} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.}
@end float

@example ocaml
let insert_queue q item =
  let new_pair = { Mpairs.car = item; cdr = Mpairs.Nil } in
  if empty_queue q
  then (
    set_front_ptr q (Mpairs.Pair new_pair);
    set_rear_ptr q (Mpairs.Pair new_pair))
  else (
    Mpairs.set_cdr (rear_ptr q) (Mpairs.Pair new_pair);
    set_rear_ptr q (Mpairs.Pair new_pair));
  q
@end example

@noindent
To delete the item at the front of the queue, we merely modify the front
pointer so that it now points at the second item in the queue, which can be
found by following the @code{cdr} pointer of the first item (see
@ref{Figure 3.21}):@footnote{If the first item is the final item in the queue, the front
pointer will be the empty list after the deletion, which will mark the queue as
empty; we needn't worry about updating the rear pointer, which will still point
to the deleted item, because @code{empty_queue} looks only at the front
pointer.}

@example ocaml
let delete_queue q =
  if empty_queue q
  then invalid_arg "DELETE called with an empty queue"
  else (
    set_front_ptr q (Mpairs.cdr (front_ptr q));
    q)
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.21}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{delete_queue q} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         +------------+                         |
            front-ptr |                         | rear-ptr
                      V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.21c,,141mm,Figure 3.21: Result of using delete_queue q on the queue of Figure 3.20.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.21c.std,,141mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{delete_queue q} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.}
@end float

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.21}Exercise 3.21:} Ben Bitdiddle decides to test the
queue implementation described above.  He loads the procedures and tries them
out, examining the queue with the section's pair printer after each operation:

@example ocaml
let q1 = make_queue ()

show (Pair (insert_queue q1 (Sym "a")))
(* "((a) a)" *)

show (Pair (insert_queue q1 (Sym "b")))
(* "((a b) b)" *)

show (Pair (delete_queue q1))
(* "((b) b)" *)

show (Pair (delete_queue q1))
(* "(() b)" *)
@end example

``It's all wrong!'' he complains.  ``The printer's response shows that the
last item is inserted into the queue twice.  And when I delete both items, the
second @code{b} is still there, so the queue isn't empty, even though it's
supposed to be.''  Eva Lu Ator suggests that Ben has misunderstood what is
happening.  ``It's not that the items are going into the queue twice,'' she
explains.  ``It's just that the plain pair printer doesn't know how to make
sense of the queue representation.  If you want to see the queue printed
correctly, you'll have to define your own print procedure for queues.'' Explain
what Eva Lu is talking about.  In particular, show why Ben's examples produce
the printed results that they do.  Define a procedure @code{print_queue} that
takes a queue as input and prints the sequence of items in the queue.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.22}Exercise 3.22:} Instead of representing a queue
as a pair of pointers, we can build a queue as an object with local state.
The local state will consist of pointers to the beginning and the end of an
ordinary list.  Thus, the @code{make_queue} constructor will have the form

@example ocaml
let make_queue () =
  let front_ptr = ⟨@r{…}⟩ in
  let rear_ptr = ⟨@r{…}⟩ in
  (* ⟨@var{definitions of internal procedures}⟩ *)
  { q_empty = ⟨@r{…}⟩
  ; q_front = ⟨@r{…}⟩
  ; q_insert = ⟨@r{…}⟩
  ; q_delete = ⟨@r{…}⟩
  }
@end example

Complete the definition of @code{make_queue} and provide implementations of the
queue operations using this representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.23}Exercise 3.23:} A @newterm{deque} (``double-ended
                                                                 queue'') is a sequence in which items can be inserted and deleted at either the
front or the rear.  Operations on deques are the constructor @code{make_deque},
the predicate @code{empty_deque}, selectors @code{front_deque} and
@code{rear_deque}, and mutators @code{front_insert_deque},
@code{rear_insert_deque}, @code{front_delete_deque},
@code{rear_delete_deque}.  Show how to represent deques using pairs, and give
