@subsubheading Representing wires

A wire in our simulation will be a computational object with two local state
variables: a @code{signal_value} (initially taken to be 0) and a collection of
@code{action_procedures} to be run when the signal changes value.  We implement
the wire in message-passing style, as a record of local closures, just as we
did with the simple bank-account object in @ref{3.1.1}:

@example ocaml
type wire =
  { get_signal : unit -> int
  ; set_signal : int -> unit
  ; add_action : (unit -> unit) -> unit
  }

let make_wire () =
  let signal_value = ref 0 in
  let action_procedures = ref [] in
  let set_my_signal new_value =
    if !signal_value <> new_value
    then (
      signal_value := new_value;
      List.iter (fun p -> p ()) !action_procedures)
  in
  let accept_action_procedure proc =
    action_procedures := proc :: !action_procedures;
    proc ()
  in
  { get_signal = (fun () -> !signal_value)
  ; set_signal = set_my_signal
  ; add_action = accept_action_procedure
  }
@end example

@noindent
The local procedure @code{set_my_signal} tests whether the new signal value
changes the signal on the wire.  If so, it runs each of the action procedures
with @code{List.iter}, which calls each of the procedures in the list in
turn.  The local procedure @code{accept_action_procedure} adds the given
procedure to the list of procedures to be run, and then runs the new procedure
once.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.31}.)

The wire record exposes the three local operations directly as its
fields.@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 155} These fields are simply the local
closures of the wire, exposed under stable names so that we can use ordinary
function-call syntax to reach the wire's local operations.  It is striking
that we can interchange the role of ``procedures'' and ``data'' in such a
simple way.  For example, writing @code{wire.get_signal} names the closure as
a piece of data stored in the wire, a value we can pass to another procedure;
calling @code{wire.get_signal ()} runs it.  The truth of the matter is that,
in a language in which we can deal with procedures as objects, there is no
fundamental difference between ``procedures'' and ``data,'' and we can choose
our names to allow us to program in whatever style we choose.}

@noindent
Wires, which have time-varying signals and may be incrementally attached to
devices, are typical of mutable objects.  We have modeled them as closures
with local state variables that are modified by assignment.  When a new wire
is created, a new set of state variables is allocated (by the @code{let}
bindings in @code{make_wire}) and a record of fresh closures over them is
built and returned.

The wires are shared among the various devices that have been connected to
them.  Thus, a change made by an interaction with one device will affect all
the other devices attached to the wire.  The wire communicates the change to
its neighbors by calling the action procedures provided to it when the
connections were established.

@subsubheading The agenda

The only thing needed to complete the simulator is @code{after_delay}.  The
idea here is that we maintain a data structure, called an @newterm{agenda},
that contains a schedule of things to do.  The agenda travels with the current
time and the three gate delays in one simulator record; the following
operations are defined for it:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{make_sim} returns a new empty simulator, given the three gate delays.

@item
@code{empty_agenda ⟨@var{sim}⟩} is true if the specified agenda is
empty.

@item
@code{first_agenda_item ⟨@var{sim}⟩} returns the first item on the
agenda.

@item
@code{remove_first_agenda_item ⟨@var{sim}⟩} modifies the agenda by
removing the first item.

@item
@code{add_to_agenda ⟨@var{sim}⟩ ⟨@var{time}⟩ ⟨@var{action}⟩}
modifies the agenda by adding the given action procedure to be run at the
specified time.

@item
@code{⟨@var{sim}⟩.current_time} returns the current simulation time.

@end itemize

@noindent
The procedure @code{after_delay} adds new elements to the simulator's agenda:

@example ocaml
let after_delay sim delay action = add_to_agenda sim (delay + sim.current_time) action
@end example

@noindent
The simulation is driven by the procedure @code{propagate}, which operates on
the simulator's agenda, executing each procedure on the agenda in sequence.
In general, as the simulation runs, new items will be added to the agenda, and
@code{propagate} will continue the simulation as long as there are items on
the agenda:

@example ocaml
let rec propagate sim =
  if empty_agenda sim
  then ()
  else (
    let action = first_agenda_item sim in
    action ();
    remove_first_agenda_item sim;
    propagate sim)
@end example

@subsubheading A sample simulation

The following procedure, which places a ``probe'' on a wire, shows the
simulator in action.  The probe tells the wire that, whenever its signal
changes value, it should record the new signal value, together with the
current time and a name that identifies the wire, on the simulator's
transcript; the transcript lines below appear in the order they were
recorded:

@example ocaml
let probe sim name wire =
  wire.add_action (fun () ->
      sim.trace
      := Printf.sprintf "%s %d  New-value = %d"
           name sim.current_time (wire.get_signal ())
           :: !(sim.trace))
@end example

@noindent
We begin by creating the simulator, specifying delays for the primitive
function boxes:

@example ocaml
let sim = make_sim ~inverter_delay:2 ~and_gate_delay:3 ~or_gate_delay:5 ()
@end example

@noindent
Now we define four wires, placing probes on two of them:

@example ocaml
let input_1 = make_wire ()
let input_2 = make_wire ()
let sum = make_wire ()
let carry = make_wire ()

probe sim "sum" sum
(* sum 0  New-value = 0 *)

probe sim "carry" carry
(* carry 0  New-value = 0 *)
@end example

@noindent
Next we connect the wires in a half-adder circuit (as in @ref{Figure 3.25}),
set the signal on @code{input_1} to 1, and run the simulation:

@example ocaml
half_adder sim input_1 input_2 sum carry
(* => ok *)

input_1.set_signal 1
(* => ok *)

propagate sim
(* sum 8  New-value = 1 *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{sum} signal changes to 1 at time 8.  We are now eight time units from
the beginning of the simulation.  At this point, we can set the signal on
@code{input_2} to 1 and allow the values to propagate:

@example ocaml
input_2.set_signal 1
(* => ok *)

propagate sim
(* carry 11  New-value = 1 *)
(* sum 16  New-value = 0 *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{carry} changes to 1 at time 11 and the @code{sum} changes to 0 at
time 16.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.31}Exercise 3.31:} The internal procedure
@code{accept_action_procedure} defined in @code{make_wire} specifies that when
a new action procedure is added to a wire, the procedure is immediately run.
Explain why this initialization is necessary.  In particular, trace through the
half-adder example in the paragraphs above and say how the system's response
would differ if we had defined @code{accept_action_procedure} as

@example ocaml
let accept_action_procedure proc =
  action_procedures := proc :: !action_procedures
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Implementing the agenda

Finally, we give details of the agenda data structure, which holds the
procedures that are scheduled for future execution.@footnote{OCaml 5.4 added
the @code{Pqueue} module, heap-backed priority queues via
@code{Pqueue.MakeMin}, with @code{pop_min} and @code{min_elt}; equal
priorities pop in unspecified order.  A priority queue could serve as an
alternative to the hand-built agenda below, but the main text keeps the
hand-built agenda because the book's deterministic time-order story is the
lesson.}

The agenda is made up of @newterm{time segments}.  Each time segment is a pair
consisting of a number (the time) and a queue (see @ref{Exercise 3.32}) that
holds the procedures that are scheduled to be run during that time segment.

@example ocaml
let make_time_segment time queue = mcons (Int time) (Pair queue)
let segment_time s = car s
let segment_queue s = pair_of (cdr s)
@end example

@noindent
A scheduled procedure travels as the @code{Proc} variant of the mutable-list
object, the variant the section set aside for the agenda alone.  We will
operate on the time-segment queues using the queue operations described
in @ref{3.3.2}.

The agenda itself is a one-dimensional table of time segments.  It differs from
the tables described in @ref{3.3.3} in that the segments will be sorted
in order of increasing time.  In addition, we keep the @newterm{current time}
(i.e., the time of the last action that was processed) in a mutable field of
the simulator record.  A newly constructed simulator has no time segments and
has a current time of 0:@footnote{The book's agenda is a headed list, like the
tables in @ref{3.3.3}; since this edition keeps the current time in its own
mutable field, the segment list needs no dummy header at all.}

@example ocaml
let empty_agenda sim = Nil = sim.segments
let first_segment sim = car sim.segments
let rest_segments sim = cdr sim.segments
@end example

@noindent
An agenda is empty if it has no time segments, which is exactly what
@code{empty_agenda} above tests.

To add an action to an agenda, we first check if the agenda is empty.  If so,
we create a time segment for the action and install this in the agenda.
Otherwise, we scan the agenda, examining the time of each segment.  If we find
a segment for our appointed time, we add the action to the associated queue.
If we reach a time later than the one to which we are appointed, we insert a
new time segment into the agenda just before it.  If we reach the end of the
agenda, we must create a new time segment at the end.

@example ocaml
let add_to_agenda sim time action =
  let cell_time segments =
    match segment_time (car segments) with
    | Int t -> t
    | _ -> invalid_arg "corrupt agenda segment"
  in
  let belongs_before segments =
    match segments with
    | Nil -> true
    | Pair _ -> time < cell_time segments
    | _ -> invalid_arg "corrupt agenda"
  in
  let make_new_time_segment time action =
    let q = Queue.make_queue () in
    ignore (Queue.insert_queue q (Proc action));
    make_time_segment time q
  in
  let rec add_to_segments segments =
    if cell_time segments = time
    then ignore (Queue.insert_queue (segment_queue (car segments)) (Proc action))
    else (
      let rest = cdr segments in
      if belongs_before rest
      then set_cdr segments (mcons (make_new_time_segment time action) rest)
      else add_to_segments rest)
  in
  if belongs_before sim.segments
  then sim.segments <- mcons (make_new_time_segment time action) sim.segments
  else add_to_segments sim.segments
@end example

@noindent
The procedure that removes the first item from the agenda deletes the item at
the front of the queue in the first time segment.  If this deletion makes the
time segment empty, we remove it from the list of segments:@footnote{Observe
that the @code{if} expression in this procedure has no @code{else} branch.
Such an ``@code{if} without an @code{else}'' is used to decide whether
to do something, rather than to select between two expressions.  In OCaml an
@code{if} with no @code{else} has type @code{unit}, the value it yields when
the predicate is false.}

@example ocaml
let remove_first_agenda_item sim =
  let q = segment_queue (first_segment sim) in
  ignore (Queue.delete_queue q);
  if Queue.empty_queue q then sim.segments <- cdr sim.segments
@end example

@noindent
The first agenda item is found at the head of the queue in the first time
segment.  Whenever we extract an item, we also update the current
time:@footnote{In this way, the current time will always be the time of the
action most recently processed.  Storing this time in its own field of the
simulator ensures that it will still be available even if the associated time
segment has been deleted.}

@example ocaml
let first_agenda_item sim =
  if empty_agenda sim
  then invalid_arg "Agenda is empty: FIRST-AGENDA-ITEM"
  else (
    let first_seg = first_segment sim in
    sim.current_time
    <- (match segment_time first_seg with
        | Int t -> t
        | _ -> assert false);
    match Queue.front_queue (segment_queue first_seg) with
    | Proc action -> action
    | _ -> invalid_arg "agenda item is not a procedure")
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.32}Exercise 3.32:} The procedures to be run during
each time segment of the agenda are kept in a queue.  Thus, the procedures for
each segment are called in the order in which they were added to the agenda
(first in, first out).  Explain why this order must be used.  In particular,
trace the behavior of an and-gate whose inputs change from 0, 1 to 1, 0 in the
same segment and say how the behavior would differ if we stored a segment's
procedures in an ordinary list, adding and removing procedures only at the
front (last in, first out).
@end quotation

@node	3.3.5, 3.4, 3.2, 3.3
@subsection Propagation of Constraints

Computer programs are traditionally organized as one-directional computations,
which perform operations on prespecified arguments to produce desired outputs.
On the other hand, we often model systems in terms of relations among
quantities.  For example, a mathematical model of a mechanical structure might
include the information that the deflection @math{d} of a metal rod is related to
the force @math{F} on the rod, the length @math{L} of the rod, the cross-sectional
area @math{A}, and the elastic modulus @math{E} via the equation
@displaymath
{dAE} \,=\, {FL.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dAE = FL
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such an equation is not one-directional.  Given any four of the quantities, we
can use it to compute the fifth.  Yet translating the equation into a
traditional computer language would force us to choose one of the quantities to
be computed in terms of the other four.  Thus, a procedure for computing the
area @math{A} could not be used to compute the deflection @math{d}, even though the
computations of @math{A} and @math{d} arise from the same
equation.@footnote{Constraint propagation first appeared in the incredibly
forward-looking @abbr{SKETCHPAD} system of Ivan @ref{Sutherland (1963)}.  A
beautiful constraint-propagation system based on the Smalltalk language was
developed by Alan @ref{Borning (1977)} at Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.  Sussman,
Stallman, and Steele applied constraint propagation to electrical circuit
analysis (@ref{Sussman and Stallman 1975}; @ref{Sussman and Steele 1980}). TK!Solver
(@ref{Konopasek and Jayaraman 1984}) is an extensive modeling environment based on
constraints.}

In this section, we sketch the design of a language that enables us to work in
terms of relations themselves.  The primitive elements of the language are
@newterm{primitive constraints}, which state that certain relations hold
between quantities.  For example, @code{adder a b c} specifies that the
quantities @math{a}, @math{b}, and @math{c} must be related by the equation
@math{{a + b = c}}, @code{multiplier x y z} expresses the constraint
@math{{xy = z}}, and @code{constant 32 y} says that the value of @math{y} must be 32.

Our language provides a means of combining primitive constraints in order to
express more complex relations.  We combine constraints by constructing
@newterm{constraint networks}, in which constraints are joined by
@newterm{connectors}.  A connector is an object that ``holds'' a value that may
participate in one or more constraints.  For example, we know that the
relationship between Fahrenheit and Celsius temperatures is
@displaymath
{9C} \,=\, {5(F - 32).}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
9C = 5(F - 32)
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such a constraint can be thought of as a network consisting of primitive adder,
multiplier, and constant constraints (@ref{Figure 3.28}).  In the figure, we
see on the left a multiplier box with three terminals, labeled @math{{m1}}, @math{{m2}},
and @math{p}.  These connect the multiplier to the rest of the network as follows:
The @math{{m1}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{C}, which will hold the Celsius
temperature.  The @math{{m2}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{w}, which is also
linked to a constant box that holds 9.  The @math{p} terminal, which the
multiplier box constrains to be the product of @math{{m1}} and @math{{m2}}, is linked to
the @math{p} terminal of another multiplier box, whose @math{{m2}} is connected to a
constant 5 and whose @math{{m1}} is connected to one of the terms in a sum.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.28}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{9C = 5(F - 32)} expressed as a constraint network.

@example
       +---------+     +---------+   v   +---------+
C -----+ m1      |  u  |      m1 +-------+ a1      |
       |    *  p +-----+ p  *    |       |    *  s +---- F
    +--+ m2      |     |      m2 +--+ +--+ a2      |
    |  +---------+     +---------+  | |  +---------+
  w |                              x| |y
    |    +-----+        +-----+     | |     +-----+
    +----+  9  |        |  5  +-----+ +-----+  32 |
         +-----+        +-----+             +-----+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.28,,142mm,Figure 3.28: The relation 9C = 5(F - 32) expressed as a constraint network.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.28.std,,142mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{{9C = 5(F - 32)}} expressed as a constraint network.}
@end float

@noindent
Computation by such a network proceeds as follows: When a connector is given a
value (by the user or by a constraint box to which it is linked), it awakens
all of its associated constraints (except for the constraint that just awakened
it) to inform them that it has a value.  Each awakened constraint box then
polls its connectors to see if there is enough information to determine a value
for a connector.  If so, the box sets that connector, which then awakens all of
its associated constraints, and so on.  For instance, in conversion between
Celsius and Fahrenheit, @math{w}, @math{x}, and @math{y} are immediately set by the
constant boxes to 9, 5, and 32, respectively.  The connectors awaken the
multipliers and the adder, which determine that there is not enough information
to proceed.  If the user (or some other part of the network) sets @math{C} to a
value (say 25), the leftmost multiplier will be awakened, and it will set @math{u}
to @math{{25 \cdot 9 = 225}}.  Then @math{u} awakens the second multiplier, which sets @math{v} to
45, and @math{v} awakens the adder, which sets @math{f} to 77.

@subsubheading Using the constraint system

To use the constraint system to carry out the temperature computation outlined
above, we first create two connectors, @code{c} and @code{f}, by calling the
constructor @code{make_connector}, and link @code{c} and @code{f} in an
appropriate network:

@example ocaml
let c = make_connector ()
let f = make_connector ()

celsius_fahrenheit_converter c f
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
The procedure that creates the network is defined as follows:

@example ocaml
let celsius_fahrenheit_converter c f =
  let u = make_connector () in
  let v = make_connector () in
  let w = make_connector () in
  let x = make_connector () in
  let y = make_connector () in
  ignore (multiplier c w u);
  ignore (multiplier v x u);
  ignore (adder v y f);
  ignore (constant 9 w);
  ignore (constant 5 x);
  ignore (constant 32 y)
@end example

@noindent
This procedure creates the internal connectors @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{w},
@code{x}, and @code{y}, and links them as shown in @ref{Figure 3.28} using the
primitive constraint constructors @code{adder}, @code{multiplier}, and
@code{constant}.  Just as with the digital-circuit simulator of
@ref{3.3.4}, expressing these combinations of primitive elements in terms of
procedures automatically provides our language with a means of abstraction for
compound objects.

To watch the network in action, we can place probes on the connectors @code{c}
and @code{f}, using a @code{probe} procedure similar to the one we used to
monitor wires in @ref{3.3.4}.  Placing a probe on a connector will
record a transcript line whenever the connector is given a value; the lines
below appear in the order they were recorded:

@example ocaml
let log = ref []

probe log "Celsius temp" c
probe log "Fahrenheit temp" f
@end example

@noindent
Next we set the value of @code{c} to 25.  (The informant argument to
@code{set_value} tells @code{c} that this directive comes from
@code{User}, a name reserved for directives from outside the network.)

@example ocaml
c.set_value 25 User
(* Probe: Celsius temp = 25 *)
(* Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 77 *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
The probe on @code{c} awakens and reports the value.  @code{c} also propagates
its value through the network as described above.  This sets @code{f} to 77,
which is reported by the probe on @code{f}.

Now we can try to set @code{f} to a new value, say 212:

@example ocaml
f.set_value 212 User
(* => Error: Contradiction (77 212) *)
@end example

@noindent
The connector complains that it has sensed a contradiction: Its value is 77,
and someone is trying to set it to 212.  If we really want to reuse the network
with new values, we can tell @code{c} to forget its old value:

@example ocaml
c.forget_value User
(* Probe: Celsius temp = ? *)
(* Probe: Fahrenheit temp = ? *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
@code{c} finds that the @code{User}, who set its value originally, is now
retracting that value, so @code{c} agrees to lose its value, as shown by the
probe, and informs the rest of the network of this fact.  This information
eventually propagates to @code{f}, which now finds that it has no reason for
continuing to believe that its own value is 77.  Thus, @code{f} also gives up
its value, as shown by the probe.

Now that @code{f} has no value, we are free to set it to 212:

@example ocaml
f.set_value 212 User
(* Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 212 *)
(* Probe: Celsius temp = 100 *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
This new value, when propagated through the network, forces @code{c} to have a
value of 100, and this is registered by the probe on @code{c}.  Notice that the
very same network is being used to compute @code{c} given @code{f} and to
compute @code{f} given @code{c}.  This nondirectionality of computation is the
distinguishing feature of constraint-based systems.

@subsubheading Implementing the constraint system

The constraint system is implemented via procedural objects with local state,
in a manner very similar to the digital-circuit simulator of
@ref{3.3.4}.  Although the primitive objects of the constraint system are
somewhat more complex, the overall system is simpler, since there is no concern
about agendas and logic delays.

The basic operations on connectors are the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.has_value ()} tells whether the connector has a value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.get_value ()} returns the connector's current value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.set_value ⟨@var{new-value}⟩ ⟨@var{informant}⟩}
indicates that the informant is requesting the connector to set its value to
the new value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.forget_value ⟨@var{retractor}⟩} tells the connector
that the retractor is requesting it to forget its value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.connect ⟨@var{new-constraint}⟩} tells the connector
to participate in the new constraint.

@end itemize

@noindent
@code{Adder} constructs an adder constraint among summand connectors @code{a1}
and @code{a2} and a @code{sum} connector.  An adder is implemented as a
procedure with local state (the @code{me} record below):

@example ocaml
let adder a1 a2 sum =
  let rec me =
    { me =
        (function
          | I_have_a_value -> process_new_value ()
          | I_lost_my_value -> process_forget_value ())
    }
  and process_new_value () =
    if a1.has_value () && a2.has_value ()
    then sum.set_value (a1.get_value () + a2.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
    else if a1.has_value () && sum.has_value ()
    then a2.set_value (sum.get_value () - a1.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
    else if a2.has_value () && sum.has_value ()
    then a1.set_value (sum.get_value () - a2.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
  and process_forget_value () =
    sum.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    a1.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    a2.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    process_new_value ()
  in
  a1.connect me;
  a2.connect me;
  sum.connect me;
  me
@end example

@noindent
@code{Adder} connects the new adder to the designated connectors and returns it
as its value.  The @code{me} field of the record represents the adder; applying
it with a request is this edition's version of the book's message dispatch
(see @ref{Footnote 155} in @ref{3.3.4}).  The connectors communicate with the
constraints by means of the procedures
@code{inform_about_value}, which tells the given constraint that the connector
has a value, and @code{inform_about_no_value}, which tells the constraint that
the connector has lost its value:

@example ocaml
let inform_about_value constraint_ = constraint_.me I_have_a_value

let inform_about_no_value constraint_ = constraint_.me I_lost_my_value
@end example

@noindent
The adder's local procedure @code{process_new_value} is called when the adder
is informed that one of its connectors has a value. The adder first checks to
see if both @code{a1} and @code{a2} have values. If so, it tells @code{sum} to
set its value to the sum of the two addends.  The @code{informant} argument to
@code{set_value} is @code{Of_constraint me}, which tags the adder object
itself.  If @code{a1}
and @code{a2} do not both have values, then the adder checks to see if perhaps
@code{a1} and @code{sum} have values.  If so, it sets @code{a2} to the
difference of these two.  Finally, if @code{a2} and @code{sum} have values,
this gives the adder enough information to set @code{a1}.  If the adder is told
that one of its connectors has lost a value, it requests that all of its
connectors now lose their values.  (Only those values that were set by this
adder are actually lost.)  Then it runs @code{process_new_value}.  The reason
for this last step is that one or more connectors may still have a value (that
is, a connector may have had a value that was not originally set by the adder),
and these values may need to be propagated back through the adder.

A multiplier is very similar to an adder. It will set its @code{product} to 0
if either of the factors is 0, even if the other factor is not known.

@example ocaml
let multiplier m1 m2 product =
  let rec me =
    { me =
        (function
          | I_have_a_value -> process_new_value ()
          | I_lost_my_value -> process_forget_value ())
    }
  and process_new_value () =
    if
      (m1.has_value () && m1.get_value () = 0)
      || (m2.has_value () && m2.get_value () = 0)
    then product.set_value 0 (Of_constraint me)
    else if m1.has_value () && m2.has_value ()
    then product.set_value (m1.get_value () * m2.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
    else if product.has_value () && m1.has_value ()
    then m2.set_value (product.get_value () / m1.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
    else if product.has_value () && m2.has_value ()
    then m1.set_value (product.get_value () / m2.get_value ()) (Of_constraint me)
  and process_forget_value () =
    product.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    m1.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    m2.forget_value (Of_constraint me);
    process_new_value ()
  in
  m1.connect me;
  m2.connect me;
  product.connect me;
  me
@end example

@noindent
A @code{constant} constructor simply sets the value of the designated
connector.  Any @code{I_have_a_value} or @code{I_lost_my_value} request sent to
the constant box will produce an error.

@example ocaml
let constant value connector =
  let handle = { me = (fun _ -> invalid_arg "Unknown request: CONSTANT") } in
  connector.connect handle;
  connector.set_value value (Of_constraint handle);
  handle
@end example

@noindent
Finally, a probe records a transcript line about the setting or unsetting of
the designated connector:

@example ocaml
let probe log name connector =
  connector.connect
    { me =
        (function
          | I_have_a_value ->
            log
            := Printf.sprintf "Probe: %s = %d" name (connector.get_value ())
               :: !log
          | I_lost_my_value ->
            log := Printf.sprintf "Probe: %s = ?" name :: !log)
    }
@end example

@subsubheading Representing connectors

A connector is represented as a procedural object with local state variables
@code{value}, the current value of the connector; @code{informant}, the
informant that set the connector's value (@code{None} until then); and
@code{constraints}, a list of the
constraints in which the connector participates.

@example ocaml
let rec make_connector () =
  let value = ref 0 in
  let informant = ref None in
  let constraints = ref [] in
  let has_value () =
    match !informant with
    | Some _ -> true
    | None -> false
  in
  let set_my_value newval setter =
    match !informant with
    | None ->
      value := newval;
      informant := Some setter;
      for_each_except setter inform_about_value !constraints
    | Some _ when !value <> newval ->
      invalid_arg (Printf.sprintf "Contradiction (%d %d)" !value newval)
    | _ -> ()
  in
  let forget_my_value retractor =
    match !informant with
    | Some who when same_informant who retractor ->
      informant := None;
      for_each_except retractor inform_about_no_value !constraints
    | _ -> ()
  in
  let connect new_constraint =
    if not (List.exists (fun c -> c == new_constraint) !constraints)
    then constraints := new_constraint :: !constraints;
    if has_value () then new_constraint.me I_have_a_value
  in
  { has_value
  ; get_value = (fun () -> !value)
  ; set_value = set_my_value
  ; forget_value = forget_my_value
  ; connect
  }
@end example

@noindent
The connector's local procedure @code{set_my_value} is called when there is a
request to set the connector's value.  If the connector does not currently have
a value, it will set its value and remember as @code{informant} the constraint
that requested the value to be set.@footnote{The @code{setter} might not be a
constraint.  In our temperature example, we used @code{User} as the
@code{setter}.}  Then the connector will notify all of its participating
constraints except the constraint that requested the value to be set.  This is
accomplished using the following iterator, which applies a designated procedure
to all items in a list except a given one:

@example ocaml
let for_each_except exception_ inform constraints =
  match exception_ with
  | User -> List.iter inform constraints
  | Of_constraint me ->
    List.iter (fun item -> if not (item == me) then inform item) constraints
@end example

@noindent
When the exception is a constraint, it is compared physically with
@code{==}, so skipping works by identity, not by structure.  If a connector is
asked to forget its value, it runs the local procedure
@code{forget_my_value}, which first checks to make sure that the request is
coming from the same object that set the value originally (the
@code{same_informant} test is again a physical comparison).  If so, the
connector informs its associated constraints about the loss of the value.

The local procedure @code{connect} adds the designated new constraint to the
list of constraints if it is not already in that list, again recognizing
membership physically.  Then, if the connector has a value, it informs the new
constraint of this fact.

The connector record exposes the local procedures as its five fields, so the
book's syntax interface needs no extra layer:
@code{c.has_value ()}, @code{c.get_value ()},
@code{c.set_value new_value informant}, @code{c.forget_value retractor}, and
@code{c.connect new_constraint} are calls on those closures.

@quotation
@strong{In this language: mutable data.}
OCaml makes mutation visible in the types.  A record field must be declared
@code{mutable} before anything can assign into it, a @code{ref} cell is read
and written only through @code{!} and @code{:=}, and the section's queues,
tables, wires, and connectors are records of closures over such cells, so an
object's state is exactly the cells its closures capture.  Aliasing, two
names for one record, shows up as physical equality with @code{==}.

@example ocaml
type cell = { mutable v : int }

let bump c = c.v <- c.v + 1
@end example

The book's mechanism still wins when identity must travel with the data: the
typed records make every mutable field visible at the call sites, but which
names share which cells is a property of the pointers, and only @code{==}
settles it.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.33}Exercise 3.33:} Using primitive multiplier,
adder, and constant constraints, define a procedure @code{averager} that takes
three connectors @code{a}, @code{b}, and @code{c} as inputs and establishes the
constraint that the value of @code{c} is the average of the values of @code{a}
and @code{b}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.34}Exercise 3.34:} Louis Reasoner wants to build a
squarer, a constraint device with two terminals such that the value of
connector @code{b} on the second terminal will always be the square of the
value @code{a} on the first terminal.  He proposes the following simple device
made from a multiplier:

@example ocaml
let squarer a b = ignore (multiplier a a b)
@end example

There is a serious flaw in this idea.  Explain.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.35}Exercise 3.35:} Ben Bitdiddle tells Louis that
one way to avoid the trouble in @ref{Exercise 3.34} is to define a squarer as a
new primitive constraint.  Fill in the missing portions in Ben's outline for a
procedure to implement such a constraint:

@example ocaml
let squarer a b =
  let rec me =
    { me =
        (function
          | I_have_a_value -> process_new_value ()
          | I_lost_my_value -> process_forget_value ())
    }
  and process_new_value () =
    if b.has_value ()
    then
      if b.get_value () < 0
      then invalid_arg "square less than 0: SQUARER"
      else ⟨@var{alternative1}⟩
    else ⟨@var{alternative2}⟩
  and process_forget_value () = ⟨@var{body1}⟩ in
  ⟨@var{rest of definition}⟩;
  me
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 3.36 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it;
the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.36}Exercise 3.36:}
Suppose we evaluate

@example ocaml
let a = make_connector ()
let b = make_connector ()

a.set_value 10 User
@end example

and that a logging constraint was connected to @code{a} before the last line,
one that appends a line to a transcript each time its @code{me} field
dispatches and each time one of its internal procedures runs.  At some moment
during the evaluation of @code{a.set_value 10 User}, the connector's local
@code{set_my_value} closure evaluates

@example ocaml
for_each_except setter inform_about_value !constraints
@end example

Trace the calls, in order: name each dispatch of the logging constraint's
@code{me}, the internal procedure each dispatch runs, and the closures over
@code{a}'s local state that hand the requests along.  Do the same for the
matching @code{a.forget_value User}.  Explain why the constraint is consulted
even though the setter, @code{User}, never is.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.37}Exercise 3.37:} The
@code{celsius_fahrenheit_converter} procedure is cumbersome when compared with
a more expression-oriented style of definition, such as

@example ocaml
let celsius_fahrenheit_converter x =
  c_add (c_div (c_mul (cv 9) x) (cv 5)) (cv 32)

let c = make_connector ()
let f = celsius_fahrenheit_converter c
@end example

Here @code{c_add}, @code{c_mul}, etc. are the ``constraint'' versions of the
arithmetic operations.  This edition's integer connectors reassociate the
book's @code{(c_mul (c_div (cv 9) (cv 5)) x)} into
@code{c_mul (cv 9) x} divided by @code{cv 5}, because the factor 9/5 has no
integer value; the network still computes in both directions.  For example,
@code{c_add} takes two connectors as
arguments and returns a connector that is related to these by an adder
constraint:

@example ocaml
let c_add x y =
  let z = make_connector () in
  ignore (adder x y z);
  z
@end example

Define analogous procedures @code{c_sub}, @code{c_mul}, @code{c_div}, and
@code{cv}
(constant value) that enable us to define compound constraints as in the
converter example above.@footnote{The expression-oriented format is convenient
because it avoids the need to name the intermediate expressions in a
computation.  Our original formulation of the constraint language is cumbersome
in the same way that many languages are cumbersome when dealing with operations
on compound data.  For example, if we wanted to compute the product
@math{{(a + b) \cdot (c + d)}}, where the variables represent vectors, we could work
in ``imperative style,'' using procedures that set the values of designated
vector arguments but do not themselves return vectors as values:

@example ocaml
v_sum a b temp1;
v_sum c d temp2;
v_prod temp1 temp2 answer
@end example

Alternatively, we could deal with expressions, using procedures that return
vectors as values, and thus avoid explicitly mentioning @code{temp1} and
@code{temp2}:

@example ocaml
let answer = v_prod (v_sum a b) (v_sum c d)
@end example

Since OCaml functions can return compound values, we can
transform our imperative-style constraint language into an expression-oriented
style as shown in this exercise.  In languages that are impoverished in
handling compound objects, such as Algol, Basic, and Pascal (unless one
explicitly uses Pascal pointer variables), one is usually stuck with the
imperative style when manipulating compound objects.  Given the advantage of
the expression-oriented format, one might ask if there is any reason to have
implemented the system in imperative style, as we did in this section.  One
reason is that the non-expression-oriented constraint language provides a
handle on constraint objects (e.g., the value of the @code{adder} procedure) as
well as on connector objects.  This is useful if we wish to extend the system
with new operations that communicate with constraints directly rather than only
indirectly via operations on connectors.  Although it is easy to implement the
expression-oriented style in terms of the imperative implementation, it is very
difficult to do the converse.}
@end quotation
